  "report_calorimetry_001": {
    "id": "report_calorimetry_001",
    "document": "Empirical Verification of Aqueous Specific Heat Capacity via Continuous Heating. Abstract: We investigated the heat capacity of 150.0 mL pure deionized water under continuous thermal input from an atmospheric burner. The initial temperature of 21.2\u00b0C rose to a final steady boiling plateau at 99.8\u00b0C. Measured enthalpy absorption yielded c = 4.179 J/g\u00b7\u00b0C, exhibiting a 0.12% relative error from literature. Methodology: Transferred 150 mL DI water into a 250 mL borosilicate beaker equipped with an immersion thermocouple. Bunsen burner ignited with constant blue flame. Recorded temperature and volume at 5-second intervals. Conclusion: The empirical sensible heat capacity of liquid water matches established IUPAC thermochemical constants within experimental uncertainty bounds.",
    "metadata": {
      "title": "Empirical Verification of Aqueous Specific Heat Capacity via Continuous Heating",
      "experiment_type": "Thermodynamics",
      "author": "Dr. Sarah Lin, MOSEB Physical Chemistry Unit",
      "source_file": "report_aqueous_specific_heat.json"
    },
